WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:02.772
- I had a good year last
year, but am I successful?

00:00:02.772 --> 00:00:04.046
And the answer, is no.

00:00:04.046 --> 00:00:05.208
I don't feel I am,

00:00:05.208 --> 00:00:08.216
because I am trying to build a
world that doesn't exist yet.

00:00:08.216 --> 00:00:10.295
When you're driving in your car,

00:00:10.295 --> 00:00:13.201
and you get a text, and
your phone goes beep,

00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:14.264
we hate email, true.

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:16.729
We love the beep, the buzz, the ding.

00:00:16.729 --> 00:00:18.662
- Have you gone and talked to

00:00:18.662 --> 00:00:20.735
a company that's been in trouble,

00:00:20.735 --> 00:00:22.933
and then spoken to their team,

00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:24.597
and then checked in on them

00:00:24.597 --> 00:00:27.023
after you've spoke to
their leadership team,

00:00:27.023 --> 00:00:28.723
and what did that look like?

00:00:28.723 --> 00:00:30.432
Did you notice a noticeable change?

00:00:30.432 --> 00:00:31.951
- You mean, does my (audio cuts out) work?

00:00:31.951 --> 00:00:33.473
(audience laughing)

00:00:33.473 --> 00:00:34.724
- Hello, Believe Nation!

00:00:34.724 --> 00:00:36.981
I'm Evan Carmichael,
my one word is believe,

00:00:36.981 --> 00:00:39.424
and I believe that
entrepreneurs are going to solve

00:00:39.424 --> 00:00:41.477
all of the world's major problems.

00:00:41.477 --> 00:00:42.768
So, to help you on your journey,

00:00:42.768 --> 00:00:43.838
today, we're going to learn from

00:00:43.838 --> 00:00:45.733
author and speaker, Simon Sinek,

00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:49.268
and my take on his top 10
rules for success, volume two.

00:00:49.268 --> 00:00:51.489
Rule number five is my personal favorite,

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:53.771
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:53.771 --> 00:00:55.517
And as always, as you're listening,

00:00:55.517 --> 00:00:57.257
if something really resonates with you,

00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:59.404
there's a message that
really holds true to you,

00:00:59.404 --> 00:01:00.958
please leave it down in the comments below

00:01:00.958 --> 00:01:02.430
and put it in quotes

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.008
so that other people can be inspired.

00:01:04.008 --> 00:01:05.456
And when you write it down,

00:01:05.456 --> 00:01:08.537
it's much more likely to
stick with yourself as well.

00:01:08.537 --> 00:01:09.554
Enjoy.

00:01:09.554 --> 00:01:12.170
(loud whoosh)

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:15.837
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:01:22.431 --> 00:01:23.514
- People are always talking about

00:01:23.514 --> 00:01:25.941
visions and missions and all this stuff.

00:01:25.941 --> 00:01:28.424
And, when people ask me,

00:01:28.424 --> 00:01:31.045
"What example should I
look to, what company?"

00:01:31.045 --> 00:01:35.783
I'm like, here's an organization
with a vision, a cause.

00:01:35.783 --> 00:01:37.998
It was founded with a cause.

00:01:37.998 --> 00:01:40.210
It's an entrepreneurial venture.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:41.684
America is an experiment.

00:01:41.684 --> 00:01:42.804
It's an entrepreneurial venture

00:01:42.804 --> 00:01:45.195
where a bunch of people
got together and decided

00:01:45.195 --> 00:01:48.028
we needed to start our own country

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:50.725
because there were certain obstacles

00:01:50.725 --> 00:01:52.783
that were getting in the way
of the vision that we had

00:01:52.783 --> 00:01:55.948
of a better kind of country,
a kind of company, right?

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:58.082
And they stated it right
out of the beginning,

00:01:58.082 --> 00:01:59.479
"All men are created equal,"

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:01.418
endowed with these inalienable rights,

00:02:01.418 --> 00:02:02.970
amongst which include life, liberty,

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.380
and the pursuit of happiness.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.818
And it's not just a competitive statement,

00:02:07.818 --> 00:02:08.992
like, "To be the best.

00:02:08.992 --> 00:02:10.299
"To be the most respected."

00:02:10.299 --> 00:02:11.469
That's not what it was.

00:02:11.469 --> 00:02:13.201
And I'm amazed how many companies

00:02:13.201 --> 00:02:16.186
start their visions or
missions with those,

00:02:16.186 --> 00:02:18.519
that terribly egocentric language.

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:20.037
It was an ideal.

00:02:20.037 --> 00:02:21.889
And the amazing thing is

00:02:21.889 --> 00:02:24.503
we've been good at it and
bad at it in our history,

00:02:24.503 --> 00:02:27.420
but it's endured for 240 plus years

00:02:29.002 --> 00:02:31.222
because we fundamentally believe

00:02:31.222 --> 00:02:33.254
that we are at our best
when we're pursuing that.

00:02:33.254 --> 00:02:34.552
But it is an ideal.

00:02:34.552 --> 00:02:37.760
We will never achieve
all people are equal,

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.685
but we will die trying,
and that's the point.

00:02:40.685 --> 00:02:42.244
And it's the same for a company,

00:02:42.244 --> 00:02:44.560
which is true vision inside a company

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.751
is something that has nothing
to do with your product.

00:02:46.751 --> 00:02:49.199
It is an ideal to which you will

00:02:49.199 --> 00:02:51.548
attempt to build and advance that ideal

00:02:51.548 --> 00:02:53.452
through your company with your product.

00:02:53.452 --> 00:02:56.253
You will never achieve the
ideal but you'll die trying.

00:02:56.253 --> 00:02:59.041
And this is what gives our work meaning.

00:02:59.041 --> 00:03:02.218
This is what gives our lives purpose.

00:03:02.218 --> 00:03:04.007
The difference between a vision and a goal

00:03:04.007 --> 00:03:05.049
is the finish line.

00:03:05.049 --> 00:03:07.481
A goal is 26.2 miles.

00:03:07.481 --> 00:03:09.219
You can simply count the metrics

00:03:09.219 --> 00:03:11.459
and know when you completed your goal.

00:03:11.459 --> 00:03:14.025
A vision is having a crystal clear sense

00:03:14.025 --> 00:03:15.326
of what the finish line looks like

00:03:15.326 --> 00:03:17.663
but no idea of how far away it is.

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:21.718
And the reality is, you
will spend your entire life

00:03:21.718 --> 00:03:23.529
never actually crossing the finish line,

00:03:23.529 --> 00:03:25.762
but the joy that every
marathon you complete

00:03:25.762 --> 00:03:27.264
you feel like you're getting closer

00:03:27.264 --> 00:03:29.008
and every milestone that you accomplish

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:30.094
makes you feel like you're getting

00:03:30.094 --> 00:03:31.501
closer and closer to the ideal,

00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:33.523
and this is what gives our
life and our work meaning.

00:03:33.523 --> 00:03:35.964
Success is an elusive thing, right?

00:03:35.964 --> 00:03:37.485
What is it?

00:03:37.485 --> 00:03:39.226
And I think it's very interesting

00:03:39.226 --> 00:03:40.651
that if most people
kind of define success,

00:03:40.651 --> 00:03:43.650
"Well, it means you've
made X amount of dollars,"

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.983
but if you make X amount of
dollars but you spend more,

00:03:45.983 --> 00:03:47.243
are you successful?

00:03:47.243 --> 00:03:49.264
Or, "Well, it means you
come home happy every day."

00:03:49.264 --> 00:03:52.689
Okay, how do you know when you're happy?

00:03:52.689 --> 00:03:54.799
So I think success is a funny thing,

00:03:54.799 --> 00:03:56.487
which is we all seem to pursue it,

00:03:56.487 --> 00:03:57.567
but we don't know how to measure it

00:03:57.567 --> 00:03:59.493
or actually how to define it.

00:03:59.493 --> 00:04:02.088
So how do you pursue something
that you can't measure?

00:04:02.088 --> 00:04:03.088
Fascinating.

00:04:03.932 --> 00:04:07.211
So when people say to me,
"How do you measure success?"

00:04:07.211 --> 00:04:10.260
the question we all have to
ask ourselves, am I successful?

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:11.712
I don't know.

00:04:11.712 --> 00:04:14.471
I had a good year last year.

00:04:14.471 --> 00:04:15.304
And what does that mean?

00:04:15.304 --> 00:04:16.262
Does it mean I made a lot of money?

00:04:16.262 --> 00:04:17.220
Does it mean I was really happy?

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.274
Well, I'll let you decide.

00:04:19.274 --> 00:04:21.971
Maybe neither, maybe both.

00:04:21.971 --> 00:04:24.203
I had a good year last
year, but am I successful?

00:04:24.203 --> 00:04:25.687
And the answer, is no.

00:04:25.687 --> 00:04:27.160
I don't feel I am,

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:30.224
because I am trying to build a
world that doesn't exist yet.

00:04:30.224 --> 00:04:32.157
I'm trying to build a world in which

00:04:32.157 --> 00:04:33.745
90% of people go home
at the end of the day

00:04:33.745 --> 00:04:35.923
feeling fulfilled by
the work that they do.

00:04:35.923 --> 00:04:38.813
So I definitely took a big
step forward towards that goal,

00:04:38.813 --> 00:04:40.755
but I'm still so far away.

00:04:40.755 --> 00:04:41.974
So somebody said to me,

00:04:41.974 --> 00:04:43.199
"How do you know if you're successful?"

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:46.274
And the answer is if it can go by itself.

00:04:46.274 --> 00:04:47.944
And so what is more interesting to me

00:04:47.944 --> 00:04:51.960
as a measurement of success
is not the markers per se.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.120
It's not the financial goal

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.209
or the size of the house
that you want to buy.

00:04:57.209 --> 00:04:58.211
Those are nice things.

00:04:58.211 --> 00:04:59.419
Go for it.

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:01.667
But those are not measurements of success.

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:05.208
Those are just nice things
to collect along the way.

00:05:05.208 --> 00:05:06.235
For me, it's momentum.

00:05:06.235 --> 00:05:08.752
I want to measure momentum, which is,

00:05:08.752 --> 00:05:09.966
when something is moving

00:05:09.966 --> 00:05:11.514
and you start to see it lose momentum,

00:05:11.514 --> 00:05:13.172
you're like, "Uh-oh, give it a push,"

00:05:13.172 --> 00:05:14.923
because if you don't give it
a push it's going to stop,

00:05:14.923 --> 00:05:17.710
and an object in stasis is
much harder to get going.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:20.696
It requires a lot more energy
to get something started

00:05:20.696 --> 00:05:22.457
than it does to keep it going, right?

00:05:22.457 --> 00:05:25.268
And so if you don't let it
stop and you can keep it going,

00:05:25.268 --> 00:05:26.386
it still might slow down down there,

00:05:26.386 --> 00:05:28.235
but you can get it
going again much easier.

00:05:28.235 --> 00:05:30.203
And for me, the opportunity
is to get the ball rolling

00:05:30.203 --> 00:05:32.536
faster and faster and
faster and faster and faster

00:05:32.536 --> 00:05:33.550
and bigger and bigger and bigger.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:34.739
It's like a snowball.

00:05:34.739 --> 00:05:36.518
And my responsibility is,

00:05:36.518 --> 00:05:37.845
because it's not rolling downhill yet,

00:05:37.845 --> 00:05:39.225
it's not on automatic yet,

00:05:39.225 --> 00:05:41.999
I need to still keep it going
and find that critical mass

00:05:41.999 --> 00:05:43.727
where it can go (imitates explosion)

00:05:43.727 --> 00:05:45.726
And at the point it can
go by itself without me,

00:05:45.726 --> 00:05:47.755
then I will find something else to do.

00:05:47.755 --> 00:05:49.543
And that may not happen in my lifetime.

00:05:49.543 --> 00:05:51.797
I think we must all stop measuring

00:05:51.797 --> 00:05:53.996
promotions, salaries, and these things,

00:05:53.996 --> 00:05:56.753
but rather measure the
momentum of your career.

00:05:56.753 --> 00:05:57.982
Does my career have momentum?

00:05:57.982 --> 00:05:59.679
Can I see it moving in
the right direction?

00:05:59.679 --> 00:06:02.012
Can I see it gathering mass?

00:06:03.224 --> 00:06:04.954
Can I see that it's becoming

00:06:04.954 --> 00:06:06.937
easier for me to keep the momentum,

00:06:06.937 --> 00:06:10.213
it's becoming easier for me to
grow the size of this thing,

00:06:10.213 --> 00:06:11.487
it's requiring less effort?

00:06:11.487 --> 00:06:12.967
That's the thing we need to measure.

00:06:12.967 --> 00:06:14.487
That's the thing that we
need to be cognizant of,

00:06:14.487 --> 00:06:15.720
which is the momentum of our careers,

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:19.049
not just the markers that
we think define our success.

00:06:19.049 --> 00:06:23.132
I did a little experiment
with a homeless person.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:25.457
Not like on them.

00:06:25.457 --> 00:06:26.469
It's not like electrodes.

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:27.766
(audience laughs)

00:06:27.766 --> 00:06:30.516
With them, voluntarily helped me.

00:06:32.193 --> 00:06:35.526
Because the whole idea of giving, right?

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:37.784
You've all walked down the street

00:06:37.784 --> 00:06:39.706
and you've all seen someone begging,

00:06:39.706 --> 00:06:42.013
and you either have or haven't

00:06:42.013 --> 00:06:44.418
thrown a few pennies in their cup.

00:06:44.418 --> 00:06:46.668
When you do, you feel good.

00:06:47.816 --> 00:06:49.554
You bought that feeling.

00:06:49.554 --> 00:06:53.187
That is a legitimate
commercial transaction.

00:06:53.187 --> 00:06:54.522
Commercial transactions are defined as

00:06:54.522 --> 00:06:56.284
the exchange of consideration.

00:06:56.284 --> 00:06:58.163
There was an exchange
of consideration here.

00:06:58.163 --> 00:06:59.526
You gave money.

00:06:59.526 --> 00:07:01.469
You got the feeling of goodwill.

00:07:01.469 --> 00:07:02.802
You paid for that feeling.

00:07:02.802 --> 00:07:04.670
If you didn't give money,

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:08.240
you either feel nothing or you feel bad.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:11.715
You can't feel good by not giving.

00:07:11.715 --> 00:07:13.448
You pay for that feeling.

00:07:13.448 --> 00:07:14.569
So now the question is,

00:07:14.569 --> 00:07:17.484
how is that person encouraging us to give?

00:07:17.484 --> 00:07:21.197
The joke is, they act like
every corporation in the world.

00:07:21.197 --> 00:07:22.747
They talk about themselves.

00:07:22.747 --> 00:07:25.257
"Me, me, me, me, me, me, me."

00:07:25.257 --> 00:07:26.090
Right?

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:29.579
They sit there with their
little outdoor advertising,

00:07:29.579 --> 00:07:31.642
little sign, right?

00:07:31.642 --> 00:07:33.745
And it says, "I'm homeless.

00:07:33.745 --> 00:07:34.717
"I'm hungry.

00:07:34.717 --> 00:07:35.726
"I've got 12 kids.

00:07:35.726 --> 00:07:36.739
"I'm a veteran.

00:07:36.739 --> 00:07:37.572
"God bless."

00:07:37.572 --> 00:07:39.213
They got it all in there,
trying to appeal to somebody,

00:07:39.213 --> 00:07:43.250
the religious vote, the veteran
vote, child sympathizers,

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:45.906
surround yourself with lots
of pets, go for that one, too.

00:07:45.906 --> 00:07:47.016
Right?

00:07:47.016 --> 00:07:51.164
All in an attempt to get
something from someone.

00:07:51.164 --> 00:07:54.067
Takers, not givers, right?

00:07:54.067 --> 00:07:55.280
All about me.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:57.050
Well, what do corporations do?

00:07:57.050 --> 00:07:58.014
"We've added more RAM.

00:07:58.014 --> 00:07:59.009
"We've added more ROM.

00:07:59.009 --> 00:08:00.453
"We've added more speed.

00:08:00.453 --> 00:08:01.475
"This one is number one.

00:08:01.475 --> 00:08:02.473
"We're the biggest. We're the best.

00:08:02.473 --> 00:08:04.327
"We've been around since 1969.

00:08:04.327 --> 00:08:05.210
"We're better than them.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:06.043
"We're faster than them.

00:08:06.043 --> 00:08:07.136
"We're more efficient than that one.

00:08:07.136 --> 00:08:10.013
"Me, me, me, me, me, me, me, me, me, me."

00:08:10.013 --> 00:08:12.354
And so even if we buy
their product, guess what?

00:08:12.354 --> 00:08:13.923
We don't really feel much.

00:08:13.923 --> 00:08:14.940
So I did this little experiment.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:18.498
I found a nice homeless lady
on the streets of New York

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:19.752
who was willing to help out.

00:08:19.752 --> 00:08:22.005
And I learned that with her
sign, which was pretty typical,

00:08:22.005 --> 00:08:24.055
"I'm homeless, I'm
hungry, blah, blah, blah,"

00:08:24.055 --> 00:08:27.472
she makes between 20 and 30 dollars a day

00:08:28.466 --> 00:08:29.775
for a day's worth of work,

00:08:29.775 --> 00:08:32.752
eight to 10 hours of sitting
there selling goodwill.

00:08:32.752 --> 00:08:34.696
Eight to 10 hours, she'll
make 20 to 30 dollars.

00:08:34.696 --> 00:08:37.192
30 dollars is considered a good day.

00:08:37.192 --> 00:08:38.960
I changed her sign,

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:43.127
and the new sign made her
40 dollars in two hours.

00:08:44.907 --> 00:08:46.478
And then she left.

00:08:46.478 --> 00:08:48.921
(audience laughs)

00:08:48.921 --> 00:08:50.247
It's one of the reasons she's homeless,

00:08:50.247 --> 00:08:52.076
is 'cause she's decided
that she only needs

00:08:52.076 --> 00:08:54.412
20 to 30 dollars a day to live.

00:08:54.412 --> 00:08:57.443
If she'd stayed, she would've made $150.

00:08:57.443 --> 00:09:00.222
The point is, she made
40 bucks in two hours.

00:09:00.222 --> 00:09:02.420
What did the sign say?

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:05.943
The sign said, "If you
only give once a month,

00:09:05.943 --> 00:09:08.203
"please think of me next time."

00:09:08.203 --> 00:09:10.000
It has nothing to do with the taker.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.432
It has everything to do with the giver.

00:09:12.432 --> 00:09:14.701
And what are the objections people give

00:09:14.701 --> 00:09:17.039
when they don't give?

00:09:17.039 --> 00:09:18.976
"I can't give to everyone.

00:09:18.976 --> 00:09:20.921
"How do I know that they really need it?"

00:09:20.921 --> 00:09:22.456
And so I addressed both those concerns.

00:09:22.456 --> 00:09:23.828
"I know you can't give to everyone,

00:09:23.828 --> 00:09:26.925
"so if you only give once a
month, my cause is legitimate.

00:09:26.925 --> 00:09:30.489
"I will still be here when
you're ready to give."

00:09:30.489 --> 00:09:31.753
40 bucks, two hours.

00:09:31.753 --> 00:09:34.545
Make it about them, not about you.

00:09:34.545 --> 00:09:38.712
The fact of the matter is
100% of customers are people,

00:09:40.278 --> 00:09:43.470
and 100% of clients are people,

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:47.008
and 100% of employees are people.

00:09:47.008 --> 00:09:48.468
I don't care how good your product is.

00:09:48.468 --> 00:09:49.791
I don't care how good your marketing is.

00:09:49.791 --> 00:09:51.510
I don't care how good your design is.

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:53.084
If you don't understand people,

00:09:53.084 --> 00:09:55.743
you don't understand business.

00:09:55.743 --> 00:09:58.331
We are social animals,
we are human beings,

00:09:58.331 --> 00:10:00.232
and our survival depends on

00:10:00.232 --> 00:10:02.701
our ability to form
trusting relationships.

00:10:02.701 --> 00:10:06.868
Do you ever watch Deadliest
Catch on the Discovery Channel?

00:10:08.491 --> 00:10:10.008
I was flipping through channels one night,

00:10:10.008 --> 00:10:11.238
and Deadliest Catch came on.

00:10:11.238 --> 00:10:15.405
And on this episode, just random,
they were in a huge storm.

00:10:16.681 --> 00:10:19.968
Now, for those of you who
don't know Deadliest Catch,

00:10:19.968 --> 00:10:22.498
they take these crab fishing
boats out in the Bering Sea,

00:10:22.498 --> 00:10:24.415
which is like terrible,

00:10:25.963 --> 00:10:29.186
and they put cameras
on them, and we watch.

00:10:29.186 --> 00:10:31.675
(audience laughs)

00:10:31.675 --> 00:10:33.302
The reason that's, I guess, significant

00:10:33.302 --> 00:10:35.269
is because these crab fishermen have,

00:10:35.269 --> 00:10:39.704
I think, one of the top five
deadliest jobs in the world.

00:10:39.704 --> 00:10:41.019
I don't know what the exact number is,

00:10:41.019 --> 00:10:45.697
but dozens of fishermen
die every year doing this.

00:10:45.697 --> 00:10:50.611
We apparently find that
entertaining, which it actually is.

00:10:50.611 --> 00:10:53.206
So they have cameras only
on five or six of the ships

00:10:53.206 --> 00:10:54.508
even though there are
many, many, many ships

00:10:54.508 --> 00:10:56.438
that go out fishing every season.

00:10:56.438 --> 00:10:58.745
And they don't really come
into proximity with each other

00:10:58.745 --> 00:11:00.658
because the ocean is huge.

00:11:00.658 --> 00:11:02.521
And they usually sabotage each other

00:11:02.521 --> 00:11:03.686
and give each other false information

00:11:03.686 --> 00:11:04.554
'cause they're all competitors.

00:11:04.554 --> 00:11:06.020
They're all looking to get the crabs

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:08.418
and make sure that they find
them and somebody else doesn't.

00:11:08.418 --> 00:11:09.421
It's business, right?

00:11:09.421 --> 00:11:10.565
It's just business. It's okay.

00:11:10.565 --> 00:11:12.709
We all do the same thing
in our own companies.

00:11:12.709 --> 00:11:17.275
And in this one episode, this
big, huge storm was so violent

00:11:17.275 --> 00:11:19.504
that they had to bring all the pots,

00:11:19.504 --> 00:11:21.424
which are the big cages that
they catch the crabs in,

00:11:21.424 --> 00:11:24.490
they had to bring all
the pots back on the boat

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:25.705
and wait out the storm.

00:11:25.705 --> 00:11:27.238
And just be dumb luck,

00:11:27.238 --> 00:11:30.950
one of the boats that had cameras on it

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:35.199
was in proximity of a boat
that didn't have cameras on it.

00:11:35.199 --> 00:11:36.266
And so they filmed,

00:11:36.266 --> 00:11:37.539
they had secured all
their puts on the deck,

00:11:37.539 --> 00:11:40.461
and so they started
filming the other boat.

00:11:40.461 --> 00:11:44.497
And they filmed a guy climbing
on the outside of the cage,

00:11:44.497 --> 00:11:45.997
securing the pots.

00:11:47.523 --> 00:11:51.227
And all of a sudden, a huge
wave hits the side of the boat

00:11:51.227 --> 00:11:53.902
and the guy is not there anymore.

00:11:53.902 --> 00:11:56.006
And the people on the boat with
the cameras start screaming,

00:11:56.006 --> 00:11:58.180
"Man overboard, man
overboard, man overboard."

00:11:58.180 --> 00:12:00.242
And they turn their boat

00:12:00.242 --> 00:12:03.685
towards where they think he might be.

00:12:03.685 --> 00:12:05.485
He's a stranger, they don't know him,

00:12:05.485 --> 00:12:07.424
they don't know the crew
members of the other boat,

00:12:07.424 --> 00:12:11.857
and yet they react, and
they turn towards him.

00:12:11.857 --> 00:12:14.200
And they find him in the drink.

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:15.536
And for those of you

00:12:15.536 --> 00:12:17.466
who don't understand
how dangerous this is,

00:12:17.466 --> 00:12:19.352
the water is so cold if
you're in the water for,

00:12:19.352 --> 00:12:21.527
I think that it's a minute or a minute 30,

00:12:21.527 --> 00:12:24.418
hypothermia will set in and you'd die.

00:12:24.418 --> 00:12:28.150
And they come upon him,
and he's screaming,

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:30.791
"Don't let me die, don't let me die."

00:12:30.791 --> 00:12:34.045
And they pull him aboard,
not out of the woods yet,

00:12:34.045 --> 00:12:37.227
they strip off his clothes
because it's wet and cold,

00:12:37.227 --> 00:12:38.943
and they wrap blankets around him

00:12:38.943 --> 00:12:42.168
to prevent hypothermia from setting in.

00:12:42.168 --> 00:12:44.147
And he survives.

00:12:44.147 --> 00:12:46.292
And it's overwhelming.

00:12:46.292 --> 00:12:48.542
And the captain comes down,

00:12:50.414 --> 00:12:53.936
and this is all, you
can go watch it on TV.

00:12:53.936 --> 00:12:57.970
The captain comes down
and he hugs this stranger,

00:12:57.970 --> 00:13:00.466
this young man, his
competitor, he hugs this guy

00:13:00.466 --> 00:13:02.383
as if he's his own son.

00:13:04.798 --> 00:13:05.631
I lost it.

00:13:06.891 --> 00:13:08.558
Everybody is crying.

00:13:10.251 --> 00:13:14.537
And you realize what happened
here was a human interaction,

00:13:14.537 --> 00:13:16.741
and the reason that they
risked their own lives

00:13:16.741 --> 00:13:17.947
to help this other person

00:13:17.947 --> 00:13:19.291
even though they spend every other day

00:13:19.291 --> 00:13:21.416
trying to get ahead and sabotage

00:13:21.416 --> 00:13:22.665
is because at the end of the day,

00:13:22.665 --> 00:13:24.297
they're all crab fishermen,

00:13:24.297 --> 00:13:26.197
and they know something about each other,

00:13:26.197 --> 00:13:27.325
and they know something about

00:13:27.325 --> 00:13:30.950
the risk that they all take to do this.

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:33.033
And when push comes to shove,

00:13:33.033 --> 00:13:35.682
they will put themselves
out there to help each other

00:13:35.682 --> 00:13:38.681
for no other reason than they get it.

00:13:38.681 --> 00:13:40.602
They're one and the same.

00:13:40.602 --> 00:13:42.254
I will promise you

00:13:42.254 --> 00:13:46.668
that every single member
of that crew that day

00:13:46.668 --> 00:13:49.568
went home with a feeling of fulfillment.

00:13:49.568 --> 00:13:54.034
I promise you that every single
person on that crew that day

00:13:54.034 --> 00:13:57.981
felt more good in their
hearts and in their jobs

00:13:57.981 --> 00:14:02.870
than the richest day that
they've ever pulled in.

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:03.971
My question is,

00:14:03.971 --> 00:14:07.901
what are you doing to help
the person next to you?

00:14:07.901 --> 00:14:12.023
Don't you want to wake up and
go to work for the only reason

00:14:12.023 --> 00:14:14.508
that you can do something
good for someone else?

00:14:14.508 --> 00:14:17.024
Wouldn't you want them to do that for you?

00:14:17.024 --> 00:14:19.247
I'm a lover of creative people,

00:14:19.247 --> 00:14:21.664
and so any sort of expression

00:14:22.671 --> 00:14:25.503
of how you see the world and
with different terminology

00:14:25.503 --> 00:14:26.778
is fascinating to me.

00:14:26.778 --> 00:14:29.971
And so even though I
myself am a photographer,

00:14:29.971 --> 00:14:31.527
so I have that visual aspect,

00:14:31.527 --> 00:14:33.457
I'm a huge fan of modern dance

00:14:33.457 --> 00:14:36.734
and spend a lot of time with
dancers and in the dance world

00:14:36.734 --> 00:14:41.498
and have tried my hand at
choreographer just to see.

00:14:41.498 --> 00:14:45.415
I'm not good, but I like
the idea of trying it.

00:14:47.927 --> 00:14:49.743
So for me, it's about perspective,

00:14:49.743 --> 00:14:52.497
which is when you hang out with dancers

00:14:52.497 --> 00:14:54.254
and you learn to dance a little bit

00:14:54.254 --> 00:14:55.494
or you learn to choreograph a little bit

00:14:55.494 --> 00:14:57.294
or you learn to paint a little bit,

00:14:57.294 --> 00:15:01.461
I'm not a painter, but I
painted a painting recently,

00:15:03.078 --> 00:15:04.542
it's like chaos theory.

00:15:04.542 --> 00:15:06.970
Everything is connected.

00:15:06.970 --> 00:15:08.229
We conveniently divide up our lives,

00:15:08.229 --> 00:15:09.196
like, "Here's my personal life.

00:15:09.196 --> 00:15:10.779
"Here's my professional life.

00:15:10.779 --> 00:15:11.737
"Here's my social life.

00:15:11.737 --> 00:15:12.785
"I'm looking to find balance."

00:15:12.785 --> 00:15:15.251
It's just you, and all
the same things apply.

00:15:15.251 --> 00:15:16.675
And so if you're good here,

00:15:16.675 --> 00:15:18.769
you can apply what you
learn here to there.

00:15:18.769 --> 00:15:20.038
And so when you learn

00:15:20.038 --> 00:15:22.472
how things interconnect
and people interconnect

00:15:22.472 --> 00:15:24.979
and how human relationships
work and presence.

00:15:24.979 --> 00:15:27.297
You want to learn about
presence, take a dance class.

00:15:27.297 --> 00:15:30.272
You learn all about how to
present yourself and be forwards.

00:15:30.272 --> 00:15:31.575
You're going to take an acting class

00:15:31.575 --> 00:15:32.790
and learn how to present your speech.

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:33.946
People say, "Simon, how
did you learn this?"

00:15:33.946 --> 00:15:36.781
It's like, I'm exposed to all of this.

00:15:36.781 --> 00:15:38.083
So the tools I've learned

00:15:38.083 --> 00:15:39.717
have just mainly been
different perspectives

00:15:39.717 --> 00:15:42.027
on how other people use
their creative talents

00:15:42.027 --> 00:15:45.295
to see the world, and if I can
get little pieces of those,

00:15:45.295 --> 00:15:47.681
they help me in many, many different ways.

00:15:47.681 --> 00:15:51.454
Imagine we're standing
in a big empty room,

00:15:51.454 --> 00:15:53.247
and we're standing in one corner

00:15:53.247 --> 00:15:55.738
and I gave you simple instruction.

00:15:55.738 --> 00:15:58.988
I want you to go to that
corner in a straight line.

00:15:58.988 --> 00:15:59.821
Right?

00:15:59.821 --> 00:16:00.712
Off you go.

00:16:00.712 --> 00:16:01.990
No big deal, right?

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:04.458
Without telling you, I slip
a chair in front of you.

00:16:04.458 --> 00:16:05.705
What do you do?

00:16:05.705 --> 00:16:07.650
You go around the chair.

00:16:07.650 --> 00:16:08.998
Now you just disobeyed
what I told you to do.

00:16:08.998 --> 00:16:11.236
I told you to go to that
corner in a straight line.

00:16:11.236 --> 00:16:13.514
But this is the amazing
thing about human beings,

00:16:13.514 --> 00:16:15.800
which is when we're given
a clear destination,

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:18.906
we use our own creativity and
our own sense of innovation

00:16:18.906 --> 00:16:20.712
and our own problem solving abilities

00:16:20.712 --> 00:16:22.669
to overcome obstacles to
get to the destination.

00:16:22.669 --> 00:16:23.502
In other words,

00:16:23.502 --> 00:16:26.466
the destination is more
important than the route.

00:16:26.466 --> 00:16:27.748
We're flexible about the route.

00:16:27.748 --> 00:16:29.255
We're obsessed with the destination.

00:16:29.255 --> 00:16:30.088
Reset.

00:16:30.088 --> 00:16:31.205
We're standing in the corner together

00:16:31.205 --> 00:16:32.774
and I give you a simple instruction.

00:16:32.774 --> 00:16:35.195
Go somewhere in this
room in a straight line.

00:16:35.195 --> 00:16:36.249
And you say to me, "Well,
where do you want me to go?"

00:16:36.249 --> 00:16:37.082
I'm like, "I don't know.

00:16:37.082 --> 00:16:38.759
"You're smart. Figure it out.

00:16:38.759 --> 00:16:39.741
"Go in a straight line."

00:16:39.741 --> 00:16:41.742
And so you pick a point
and you start walking,

00:16:41.742 --> 00:16:43.163
and without telling you, I
put a chair in front of you.

00:16:43.163 --> 00:16:44.227
What do you do?

00:16:44.227 --> 00:16:45.466
You come to a grinding halt.

00:16:45.466 --> 00:16:46.504
I say, "What did you stop for?"

00:16:46.504 --> 00:16:48.738
You go, "Well, you put
a chair in front of me."

00:16:48.738 --> 00:16:52.470
Or, you'll make a sudden turn
and go in another direction.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:53.492
And this is the problem.

00:16:53.492 --> 00:16:55.230
It's the same obstacle.

00:16:55.230 --> 00:16:57.448
The difference is, when you
have a clear destination,

00:16:57.448 --> 00:17:00.245
the obstacles become easy to overcome.

00:17:00.245 --> 00:17:01.499
When you don't have a clear destination,

00:17:01.499 --> 00:17:03.009
you keep coming to a grinding halt.

00:17:03.009 --> 00:17:04.200
And what we do in our companies

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:06.230
is we're counting the steps
we're taking along the route,

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:08.238
but we're never looking
at the destination.

00:17:08.238 --> 00:17:10.721
So a company says, "Made a
million dollars this year.

00:17:10.721 --> 00:17:12.154
"We were only planning on making 800,000.

00:17:12.154 --> 00:17:13.290
"We took 10 steps.

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:15.020
"We were only planning on taking eight."

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:15.994
Where are you going?

00:17:15.994 --> 00:17:17.176
"No clue."

00:17:17.176 --> 00:17:18.496
(audience laughs)

00:17:18.496 --> 00:17:19.329
We count the steps.

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:21.005
And so the point is that
people want to feel that

00:17:21.005 --> 00:17:22.300
the effort that they're exerting

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:23.946
actually are moving somewhere.

00:17:23.946 --> 00:17:26.212
And so successful measurement,
successful recognition,

00:17:26.212 --> 00:17:27.995
is not just for the steps you take.

00:17:27.995 --> 00:17:29.460
It's not just for the effort.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:30.816
It's that the effort you exerted

00:17:30.816 --> 00:17:33.181
moved us closer to where
we're trying to get to,

00:17:33.181 --> 00:17:36.039
and that get to should
be some crazy ideal.

00:17:36.039 --> 00:17:37.667
My ideal is to live in a world in which

00:17:37.667 --> 00:17:38.782
the vast majority of people

00:17:38.782 --> 00:17:41.484
wake up every single morning
inspired to go to work

00:17:41.484 --> 00:17:43.455
and fulfilled by the work that they do.

00:17:43.455 --> 00:17:46.019
And the couple of measurements that I use

00:17:46.019 --> 00:17:47.782
are if the book is selling.

00:17:47.782 --> 00:17:49.381
And by the way, people ask
me, "How many have you sold?"

00:17:49.381 --> 00:17:51.016
I have no clue.

00:17:51.016 --> 00:17:53.260
I've never asked the publisher
because I don't care.

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:54.999
I really don't care how many I've sold.

00:17:54.999 --> 00:17:56.995
What I care about is the Amazon rankings

00:17:56.995 --> 00:18:00.797
and that those are going steady
or up and not plummeting,

00:18:00.797 --> 00:18:02.952
because that means other people,

00:18:02.952 --> 00:18:04.025
'cause I don't have a publicist,

00:18:04.025 --> 00:18:05.743
I don't have a marketing
strategy on purpose.

00:18:05.743 --> 00:18:07.025
I didn't hire one of those companies

00:18:07.025 --> 00:18:08.144
to sell the book for me.

00:18:08.144 --> 00:18:10.982
And the reason is because I'm
not interested in book sales.

00:18:10.982 --> 00:18:12.796
I'm interested in spreading an idea.

00:18:12.796 --> 00:18:15.512
And so I just use that as a
metric to help me understand,

00:18:15.512 --> 00:18:18.685
"Am I marching in,"
because the more I preach,

00:18:18.685 --> 00:18:20.786
is it resonating?

00:18:20.786 --> 00:18:23.677
And so you have a couple of
these imperfect measurements

00:18:23.677 --> 00:18:26.514
that help you understand,
are you going along the way?

00:18:26.514 --> 00:18:28.301
So it's not just, "Great effort.

00:18:28.301 --> 00:18:30.011
"Look what you achieved,"

00:18:30.011 --> 00:18:31.732
because that's what we're doing now.

00:18:31.732 --> 00:18:36.180
"Our goal is to increase top
line revenues by $50 million."

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:38.251
For what reason?

00:18:38.251 --> 00:18:40.463
Which is we have to know the destination,

00:18:40.463 --> 00:18:42.630
and then we say, "Amazing.

00:18:43.688 --> 00:18:45.014
"You took us that much closer."

00:18:45.014 --> 00:18:46.079
If we go to the right,

00:18:46.079 --> 00:18:48.280
it's because we were
overcoming an obstacle.

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:50.061
If we hadn't gone to the right,

00:18:50.061 --> 00:18:51.659
we would've been stuck forever.

00:18:51.659 --> 00:18:53.217
Thank you.

00:18:53.217 --> 00:18:55.276
It's not always straight lines,

00:18:55.276 --> 00:18:58.690
it's not always straight lines,
but it's in one direction.

00:18:58.690 --> 00:19:00.255
She's pulling the cane out.

00:19:00.255 --> 00:19:01.611
Rarely are we instant experts.

00:19:01.611 --> 00:19:05.778
You may have a particular gift
or affinity towards something

00:19:06.746 --> 00:19:08.829
but you still get better.

00:19:09.782 --> 00:19:10.915
People would pay me

00:19:10.915 --> 00:19:12.532
high compliments when I started speaking,

00:19:12.532 --> 00:19:14.952
and then people who have
seen me a year or two later

00:19:14.952 --> 00:19:17.209
say that I'm even better, and I feel it.

00:19:17.209 --> 00:19:18.448
- Why is that?

00:19:18.448 --> 00:19:21.064
- Because you learn more.

00:19:21.064 --> 00:19:22.986
I think that hubris is dangerous.

00:19:22.986 --> 00:19:25.216
I think to think that
you're an expert at anything

00:19:25.216 --> 00:19:26.966
is a foolish pursuit.

00:19:28.477 --> 00:19:30.318
You're never as good as you could be.

00:19:30.318 --> 00:19:32.299
There's always room for improvement.

00:19:32.299 --> 00:19:34.469
There's always room to get better.

00:19:34.469 --> 00:19:36.729
That doesn't mean you have
to listen to all the advice.

00:19:36.729 --> 00:19:40.556
Just not necessarily
does everybody know best.

00:19:40.556 --> 00:19:43.188
But to believe that you can be better

00:19:43.188 --> 00:19:46.181
and to believe that you can offer more

00:19:46.181 --> 00:19:48.457
is a constant pursuit.

00:19:48.457 --> 00:19:51.040
I used to think being a public
speaker meant being poised

00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:53.443
and presenting in a
way that was compelling

00:19:53.443 --> 00:19:54.771
and speaking at the right pace.

00:19:54.771 --> 00:19:56.771
And that's a part of it,

00:19:58.278 --> 00:20:01.568
but I have been taking more risks lately

00:20:01.568 --> 00:20:03.909
doing things that are very unstructured

00:20:03.909 --> 00:20:05.217
and very uncomfortable.

00:20:05.217 --> 00:20:08.557
And I will now do, like if
I have an hour to speak,

00:20:08.557 --> 00:20:10.163
I'd rather speak for 20 minutes

00:20:10.163 --> 00:20:12.290
and do 40 minutes worth of questions.

00:20:12.290 --> 00:20:13.687
And who knows how that's going to go?

00:20:13.687 --> 00:20:15.685
And that, to me, is the best.

00:20:15.685 --> 00:20:17.277
So I'm a better speaker

00:20:17.277 --> 00:20:19.914
because now I'm way more
open to the unknown,

00:20:19.914 --> 00:20:23.485
where a few years ago,
that would've scared me.

00:20:23.485 --> 00:20:25.956
Dopamine is the feeling

00:20:25.956 --> 00:20:27.769
that you've found something
you're looking for,

00:20:27.769 --> 00:20:30.552
that you accomplished something
you set out to accomplish.

00:20:30.552 --> 00:20:31.555
So you know that feeling you get

00:20:31.555 --> 00:20:33.706
when you cross something
off your to do list?

00:20:33.706 --> 00:20:34.749
That's dopamine.

00:20:34.749 --> 00:20:36.166
It feels awesome.

00:20:36.166 --> 00:20:38.779
When you have a goal to hit
and you achieve that goal,

00:20:38.779 --> 00:20:40.220
you're like, "Yes!"

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:41.676
You feel like you won something, right?

00:20:41.676 --> 00:20:42.991
That's dopamine.

00:20:42.991 --> 00:20:44.507
The whole purpose of dopamine

00:20:44.507 --> 00:20:47.757
is to make sure that we get stuff done.

00:20:49.084 --> 00:20:50.957
The historical reason for dopamine,

00:20:50.957 --> 00:20:54.731
we would never eat if we only
waited 'till we got hungry,

00:20:54.731 --> 00:20:57.288
because there's no guarantee
that we would find food.

00:20:57.288 --> 00:20:59.697
So dopamine exists to help
us go looking for food.

00:20:59.697 --> 00:21:01.566
We get dopamine when we eat,

00:21:01.566 --> 00:21:03.533
which is one of the
reasons we like eating.

00:21:03.533 --> 00:21:04.527
And so when you see something

00:21:04.527 --> 00:21:06.486
that reminds you of
something that feels good,

00:21:06.486 --> 00:21:07.974
we want to do the behavior

00:21:07.974 --> 00:21:09.936
that helps us get that feeling, right?

00:21:09.936 --> 00:21:11.442
So let's say you're out
there going for a walk

00:21:11.442 --> 00:21:13.795
and you see an apple tree in the distance.

00:21:13.795 --> 00:21:15.279
You get a small hit of dopamine.

00:21:15.279 --> 00:21:18.030
And then what it does is
it focuses us on our goals,

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:20.094
and then we start walking
towards the apple tree.

00:21:20.094 --> 00:21:23.236
And as the apple tree starts
to get a little bigger,

00:21:23.236 --> 00:21:24.483
we feel like we're making progress,

00:21:24.483 --> 00:21:26.175
you get another little shot of dopamine

00:21:26.175 --> 00:21:27.431
and another little shot of dopamine

00:21:27.431 --> 00:21:30.030
until you get to the tree
and you're like, "Yes!"

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:33.494
This is why we're told you
must write down your goals.

00:21:33.494 --> 00:21:35.510
Your goals must be tangible.

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:37.312
There's a biological reason for that.

00:21:37.312 --> 00:21:39.431
We're very, very
visually-oriented animals.

00:21:39.431 --> 00:21:42.697
You have to be able to see the goal

00:21:42.697 --> 00:21:45.661
for it to biologically stay focused.

00:21:45.661 --> 00:21:46.990
If you don't write down your goals,

00:21:46.990 --> 00:21:48.216
if you can't see your goals,

00:21:48.216 --> 00:21:51.727
it's very hard to get
motivated to get inspired.

00:21:51.727 --> 00:21:54.529
For example, think
about corporate visions.

00:21:54.529 --> 00:21:57.499
A corporate vision has to
be something we can see.

00:21:57.499 --> 00:21:59.720
That's why it's called a vision.

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:01.480
You can see it, right?

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:03.746
To be the biggest, most respected,

00:22:03.746 --> 00:22:06.211
to be the fastest growing are not visions.

00:22:06.211 --> 00:22:08.473
They're nothing, right?

00:22:08.473 --> 00:22:09.559
What does that even look like?

00:22:09.559 --> 00:22:11.238
Respected by whom, your mother?

00:22:11.238 --> 00:22:13.718
Yourself, your friends, your shareholders?

00:22:13.718 --> 00:22:14.551
Who knows?

00:22:14.551 --> 00:22:15.384
What's the metric?

00:22:15.384 --> 00:22:16.217
Don't know.

00:22:16.217 --> 00:22:17.239
It's amorphous.

00:22:17.239 --> 00:22:18.726
It doesn't motivate us.

00:22:18.726 --> 00:22:20.706
Just like I can't tell you,

00:22:20.706 --> 00:22:23.978
"You'll get a bonus if you achieve more."

00:22:23.978 --> 00:22:25.737
You're going to ask me, "How much more?"

00:22:25.737 --> 00:22:28.262
I'm going to say, "More."

00:22:28.262 --> 00:22:29.216
It doesn't work.

00:22:29.216 --> 00:22:31.229
You need a tangible goal.

00:22:31.229 --> 00:22:32.417
You need a tangible goal, right?

00:22:32.417 --> 00:22:34.448
Here's a great vision.

00:22:34.448 --> 00:22:35.281
Martin Luther King.

00:22:35.281 --> 00:22:37.614
"I have a dream that one day

00:22:38.747 --> 00:22:41.225
"little black children
and little white children

00:22:41.225 --> 00:22:43.266
"play on the playground together
and hold hands together."

00:22:43.266 --> 00:22:44.677
We can imagine that.

00:22:44.677 --> 00:22:46.226
We can set our sights on that.

00:22:46.226 --> 00:22:47.726
And every time we achieve a goal

00:22:47.726 --> 00:22:49.783
and achieve a metric
and achieve a milestone

00:22:49.783 --> 00:22:52.285
that makes us feel like
we're making progress

00:22:52.285 --> 00:22:55.527
to the vision we can see, we
keep going and going and going

00:22:55.527 --> 00:22:57.673
until we achieve something remarkable.

00:22:57.673 --> 00:22:58.960
You have to be able to see it.

00:22:58.960 --> 00:22:59.793
Dopamine.

00:23:01.559 --> 00:23:02.392
Like I said,

00:23:02.392 --> 00:23:04.738
dopamine is the feeling
you get when you set out

00:23:04.738 --> 00:23:06.987
to find something you're
looking for, as well.

00:23:06.987 --> 00:23:08.534
Talked about the to do list.

00:23:08.534 --> 00:23:11.712
I came home from a trip
just a couple days ago

00:23:11.712 --> 00:23:13.290
and I had a bunch of errands to run,

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:15.191
and I wrote down a little
list of things I had to do

00:23:15.191 --> 00:23:16.441
and off I went.

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:19.217
And as I was walking past,

00:23:19.217 --> 00:23:20.976
I think it was the dry
cleaners, I don't remember,

00:23:20.976 --> 00:23:22.554
I was walking past something,

00:23:22.554 --> 00:23:23.797
I remember, "Ooh, I have to do that,"

00:23:23.797 --> 00:23:26.467
and I hadn't written it
down on my to do list.

00:23:26.467 --> 00:23:28.708
So I went in and finished
what I needed to do,

00:23:28.708 --> 00:23:29.971
and then when I came out

00:23:29.971 --> 00:23:32.767
I then wrote it to on my to do
list and then crossed it out.

00:23:32.767 --> 00:23:34.443
(audience laughs)

00:23:34.443 --> 00:23:35.756
'Cause I wanted the dopamine.

00:23:35.756 --> 00:23:36.714
It feels good.

00:23:36.714 --> 00:23:39.049
(audience laughs)

00:23:39.049 --> 00:23:41.961
Dopamine comes with a warning.

00:23:41.961 --> 00:23:45.711
Dopamine is highly,
highly, highly addictive.

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:50.802
Here are some other things
that release dopamine.

00:23:50.802 --> 00:23:54.552
Alcohol, nicotine,
gambling, your cell phone.

00:23:57.220 --> 00:23:59.430
Oh, you think I'm joking?

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:01.430
We've all been told that

00:24:02.936 --> 00:24:06.433
if you wake up in the morning
and you crave a drink,

00:24:06.433 --> 00:24:08.240
you might be an alcoholic.

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:09.314
Well, if you wake up in the morning

00:24:09.314 --> 00:24:10.425
and the first thing you
do is check your phone

00:24:10.425 --> 00:24:14.734
before you even get out of
bed, might be an addict.

00:24:14.734 --> 00:24:17.970
If you walk from room to
room in your own apartment

00:24:17.970 --> 00:24:20.000
holding your telephone,

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:22.833
(audience laughs)

00:24:24.705 --> 00:24:26.974
you might be an addict.

00:24:26.974 --> 00:24:28.700
When you're driving in your car

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:31.951
and you get a text and
your phone goes beep,

00:24:31.951 --> 00:24:33.049
we hate email, true.

00:24:33.049 --> 00:24:35.682
We love the beep, the buzz, the ding.

00:24:35.682 --> 00:24:37.423
(audience laughs)

00:24:37.423 --> 00:24:38.256
Right?

00:24:38.256 --> 00:24:39.691
You'll be there in 10 minutes,

00:24:39.691 --> 00:24:42.762
and yet you have to look at it right now.

00:24:42.762 --> 00:24:43.763
You might be an addict.

00:24:43.763 --> 00:24:45.468
And even if you read it and it says,

00:24:45.468 --> 00:24:47.513
"Are you free for dinner next Thursday?"

00:24:47.513 --> 00:24:48.987
and you have to reply immediately,

00:24:48.987 --> 00:24:52.260
you can't wait the 10 minutes,
you might be an addict.

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:53.740
And for all you Gen Ys out there

00:24:53.740 --> 00:24:55.450
who like to think that
you're better at multitasking

00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:57.246
because you grew up with the technology,

00:24:57.246 --> 00:24:59.745
then why do you keep crashing
your cars when you're texting?

00:24:59.745 --> 00:25:02.035
(audience laughs)

00:25:02.035 --> 00:25:03.408
You're not better at multitasking.

00:25:03.408 --> 00:25:05.493
You're better at getting distracted.

00:25:05.493 --> 00:25:09.997
In fact, if you look at the
statistics, ADD and ADHD,

00:25:09.997 --> 00:25:12.039
diagnoses of ADD and ADHD have risen

00:25:12.039 --> 00:25:15.017
66% in the past 10 years.

00:25:15.017 --> 00:25:18.771
ADD and ADHD is a frontal lobe disorder.

00:25:18.771 --> 00:25:20.485
Are you telling me out of nowhere

00:25:20.485 --> 00:25:23.634
66% of our youth have
a frontal lobe problem?

00:25:23.634 --> 00:25:25.030
Where did that come from?

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:27.030
No, it's a misdiagnosis.

00:25:27.985 --> 00:25:30.986
What are the symptoms of a
dopamine addiction to technology?

00:25:30.986 --> 00:25:34.891
Distractibility, inability
to get things done,

00:25:34.891 --> 00:25:38.177
easily distracted, shortness of attention.

00:25:38.177 --> 00:25:39.413
It's all the same thing.

00:25:39.413 --> 00:25:40.926
So we misdiagnose things.

00:25:40.926 --> 00:25:42.262
It's this.

00:25:42.262 --> 00:25:44.258
It's the addictive quality of dopamine.

00:25:44.258 --> 00:25:47.001
We can also get addicted to
performance in our companies

00:25:47.001 --> 00:25:48.973
when all they do is
give us numbers to hit,

00:25:48.973 --> 00:25:50.272
numbers to hit, numbers to hit,

00:25:50.272 --> 00:25:51.948
and a bonus you get, and a bonus you get,

00:25:51.948 --> 00:25:52.980
and a bonus you get.

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:54.711
All they're doing is they're
feeding us with dopamine,

00:25:54.711 --> 00:25:55.790
and we can't help ourselves.

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:57.497
All we do is want more, more, more.

00:25:57.497 --> 00:26:01.027
And it's no surprise that the
banks destroyed the economy,

00:26:01.027 --> 00:26:02.972
because one of the things we
know about a dopamine addict

00:26:02.972 --> 00:26:04.722
is they will do anything
to get another hit,

00:26:04.722 --> 00:26:06.591
sometimes at the sacrifice
of their own resources

00:26:06.591 --> 00:26:07.593
and their own relationships.

00:26:07.593 --> 00:26:10.819
Ask any alcoholic, gambling
addict, or drug addict.

00:26:10.819 --> 00:26:12.567
Ask them how their relationships are doing

00:26:12.567 --> 00:26:14.729
and if they've squandered
any of their resources.

00:26:14.729 --> 00:26:15.980
It's an addiction.

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:19.480
Dopamine is dangerous if it is unbalanced.

00:26:20.683 --> 00:26:22.080
It is hugely helpful

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:24.073
when in a comfortable and balanced system,

00:26:24.073 --> 00:26:28.191
but when unbalanced, it's
dangerous and it's destructive.

00:26:28.191 --> 00:26:32.358
There's a brilliant leader
by the name of David Marquet

00:26:33.417 --> 00:26:35.762
who wrote a book called
the Turn the Ship Around.

00:26:35.762 --> 00:26:36.728
And he had an experience

00:26:36.728 --> 00:26:39.244
as a submarine captain on the USS Santa Fe

00:26:39.244 --> 00:26:41.161
where he realized that,

00:26:42.001 --> 00:26:44.434
as he much as he knew about submarining,

00:26:44.434 --> 00:26:46.975
he'd been a submariner his whole career,

00:26:46.975 --> 00:26:49.079
that put on this new submarine,
he learned the hard way

00:26:49.079 --> 00:26:51.691
that he actually didn't know
how the submarine worked.

00:26:51.691 --> 00:26:53.471
He made an order that
nobody knew how to do it

00:26:53.471 --> 00:26:56.486
because that didn't exist on that sub.

00:26:56.486 --> 00:26:59.250
And so he realized he had no
choice but to trust his people.

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:01.414
And he went through this
transformation as a leader

00:27:01.414 --> 00:27:03.478
of telling everybody what to do

00:27:03.478 --> 00:27:07.433
to allowing people to tell
him what should be done.

00:27:07.433 --> 00:27:09.013
And I've learned a lot from him

00:27:09.013 --> 00:27:12.259
and I highly recommend his book.

00:27:12.259 --> 00:27:14.491
And I've really learned that,

00:27:14.491 --> 00:27:18.926
which is at the top of the
organization, as David says,

00:27:18.926 --> 00:27:20.701
the people have all the authority,

00:27:20.701 --> 00:27:21.742
the leaders have all the authority,

00:27:21.742 --> 00:27:24.189
but at the bottom, they
have all the context.

00:27:24.189 --> 00:27:27.280
And so you can't just
push all the context up.

00:27:27.280 --> 00:27:30.029
You have to push the authority down.

00:27:30.029 --> 00:27:32.517
And so the responsibility of
leadership is to train people,

00:27:32.517 --> 00:27:34.300
make sure that they have the skillset,

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:35.334
help build their confidence

00:27:35.334 --> 00:27:37.775
that they have the confidence
to do what needs to be done,

00:27:37.775 --> 00:27:40.041
they have to have
competence and confidence,

00:27:40.041 --> 00:27:42.072
and that's your job.

00:27:42.072 --> 00:27:43.049
That's the only job of the leader.

00:27:43.049 --> 00:27:44.630
It makes you like a parent.

00:27:44.630 --> 00:27:46.453
Make sure they have
competence and confidence.

00:27:46.453 --> 00:27:47.690
Make sure your kids get schooling

00:27:47.690 --> 00:27:49.283
and make sure that they
believe in themselves,

00:27:49.283 --> 00:27:50.866
and then leave 'em.

00:27:51.734 --> 00:27:53.542
So I've done the same thing.

00:27:53.542 --> 00:27:56.420
Instead of showing
people how I would do it,

00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:59.014
I want them to learn how it's done

00:27:59.014 --> 00:28:00.803
and feel good about themselves,

00:28:00.803 --> 00:28:03.941
and then just however they
do it is how they do it.

00:28:03.941 --> 00:28:05.984
And the result is remarkable.

00:28:05.984 --> 00:28:07.171
People feel better about coming to work.

00:28:07.171 --> 00:28:08.720
They feel like they have
something to contribute.

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:11.703
They feel more valuable as
opposed to just being told,

00:28:11.703 --> 00:28:12.714
"This is how I would do it,"

00:28:12.714 --> 00:28:15.805
or, "I'm going to do it this
way," or, "Do it my way."

00:28:15.805 --> 00:28:17.388
So yeah.

00:28:17.388 --> 00:28:20.755
Completely changing my
understanding of my job

00:28:20.755 --> 00:28:23.755
as more like a parent than a manager

00:28:26.437 --> 00:28:28.291
has had a remarkable impact.

00:28:28.291 --> 00:28:30.475
It's easy to pull things back.

00:28:30.475 --> 00:28:32.754
It's very hard to ramp things up.

00:28:32.754 --> 00:28:33.714
And I'd rather start with

00:28:33.714 --> 00:28:36.336
something that's too big and
pull it back into reality

00:28:36.336 --> 00:28:38.224
than start with something that's so easy

00:28:38.224 --> 00:28:40.891
that you can't really get it up.

00:28:41.753 --> 00:28:45.187
For example, don't set
your goals realistically.

00:28:45.187 --> 00:28:47.452
Set them entirely unrealistically.

00:28:47.452 --> 00:28:50.433
Shoot for 80 and be
disappointed when you hit 70

00:28:50.433 --> 00:28:52.021
as opposed to shooting for 20

00:28:52.021 --> 00:28:53.744
and being ecstatic when you hit 21.

00:28:53.744 --> 00:28:54.810
"We beat our goal!"

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:57.143
Yeah, but it was a low goal.

00:28:58.601 --> 00:29:02.859
I think to be frustrated
and achieving something

00:29:02.859 --> 00:29:06.430
rather than ecstatic and achieving less

00:29:06.430 --> 00:29:10.597
is a better way to live, not
to mention you achieve more.

00:29:12.434 --> 00:29:15.482
I'm a great believer in the greater good.

00:29:15.482 --> 00:29:19.666
Those things are so
big they're ridiculous.

00:29:19.666 --> 00:29:21.303
My goal is 100 year goal.

00:29:21.303 --> 00:29:26.184
It's called world peace. (blows raspberry)

00:29:26.184 --> 00:29:27.184
So daunting.

00:29:28.668 --> 00:29:29.707
But that's the idea.

00:29:29.707 --> 00:29:34.411
If I fail, and I certainly
won't achieve it in my lifetime,

00:29:34.411 --> 00:29:37.238
I'd like to think that what
I'll contribute towards that

00:29:37.238 --> 00:29:39.965
ridiculous, idealistic, nonsense goal

00:29:39.965 --> 00:29:41.529
will be more than if I say,

00:29:41.529 --> 00:29:45.760
"If I can just be happy by
myself in my little house."

00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:48.225
That's nice and all, but I like the idea

00:29:48.225 --> 00:29:50.195
of contributing to your neighbor, as well.

00:29:50.195 --> 00:29:52.461
- You speak to businesses and companies

00:29:52.461 --> 00:29:55.030
and leadership teams
and employees and stuff.

00:29:55.030 --> 00:29:57.086
Without mentioning names, I don't
want to put you on the spot,

00:29:57.086 --> 00:29:59.641
but have you gone and talked to

00:29:59.641 --> 00:30:01.734
a company that's been in trouble

00:30:01.734 --> 00:30:03.809
and then spoken to their team

00:30:03.809 --> 00:30:05.724
and then checked in on them

00:30:05.724 --> 00:30:08.035
after you've spoken to
their leadership team,

00:30:08.035 --> 00:30:09.779
and what did that look like?

00:30:09.779 --> 00:30:11.725
Did you notice a noticeable change?

00:30:11.725 --> 00:30:13.443
Did they come to you and tell you that,

00:30:13.443 --> 00:30:15.427
"This has helped our organization out

00:30:15.427 --> 00:30:19.263
"and our culture is much
improved because of it?"

00:30:19.263 --> 00:30:20.592
- You mean does my (audio cuts out) work?

00:30:20.592 --> 00:30:23.746
(audience laughs)

00:30:23.746 --> 00:30:26.746
(audience applauds)

00:30:31.806 --> 00:30:32.639
I mean--

00:30:32.639 --> 00:30:34.526
(audience laughs)

00:30:34.526 --> 00:30:35.359
sure.

00:30:37.754 --> 00:30:40.161
Here's the problem with my stuff.

00:30:40.161 --> 00:30:41.967
You got to do it,

00:30:41.967 --> 00:30:45.134
and I'm not anybody's like mom or dad.

00:30:47.439 --> 00:30:48.990
I'm not going to do it for you,

00:30:48.990 --> 00:30:51.005
and I have a very
laissez-faire approach to it.

00:30:51.005 --> 00:30:55.421
I once had a client, this
was a bunch of years ago,

00:30:55.421 --> 00:30:57.465
that said, "What guarantee do I have

00:30:57.465 --> 00:30:59.947
"that your stuff will work?"

00:30:59.947 --> 00:31:02.530
To which my answer was, "None."

00:31:04.224 --> 00:31:06.473
I've given you a tool.

00:31:06.473 --> 00:31:07.474
It's like a hammer.

00:31:07.474 --> 00:31:09.067
You can use it broadly or narrowly.

00:31:09.067 --> 00:31:10.247
You can build a table.
You can build a house.

00:31:10.247 --> 00:31:11.180
It's the same tool.

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:12.013
You can use it for marketing.

00:31:12.013 --> 00:31:15.157
You can use it to completely
revitalize your entire culture.

00:31:15.157 --> 00:31:17.926
Even though I'm going to sell
you the most beautiful hammer,

00:31:17.926 --> 00:31:18.965
I'm not going to guarantee

00:31:18.965 --> 00:31:20.701
the structural integrity of the house.

00:31:20.701 --> 00:31:21.534
Right?

00:31:21.534 --> 00:31:22.860
It's your business.

00:31:22.860 --> 00:31:24.348
You want to ignore all
my stuff, ignore it.

00:31:24.348 --> 00:31:25.439
I don't care.

00:31:25.439 --> 00:31:27.459
If your business collapses,
you know what happens to me?

00:31:27.459 --> 00:31:28.292
Nothing.

00:31:30.126 --> 00:31:31.733
I don't mean to be cold about it.

00:31:31.733 --> 00:31:34.024
Of course I want the people
I work with to do well,

00:31:34.024 --> 00:31:36.537
but it's not mine, it's theirs,

00:31:36.537 --> 00:31:38.746
and I take no emotional responsibility

00:31:38.746 --> 00:31:40.284
for the decisions they make.

00:31:40.284 --> 00:31:41.975
So yes, there are many people

00:31:41.975 --> 00:31:44.060
that I've had the
pleasure of working with,

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:45.920
some who worked for
dysfunctional organizations,

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:48.042
that went on the hard journey

00:31:48.042 --> 00:31:50.195
of completely changing the way they lead

00:31:50.195 --> 00:31:52.423
and completely revitalizing their culture,

00:31:52.423 --> 00:31:54.266
and it has great success.

00:31:54.266 --> 00:31:55.255
It's not because of me.

00:31:55.255 --> 00:31:57.238
It's because of them.

00:31:57.238 --> 00:31:59.004
At the same time, there are
many who came and were like,

00:31:59.004 --> 00:32:02.260
"What an amazing speech," and did nothing.

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:04.438
"Thanks, that was great."

00:32:04.438 --> 00:32:06.938
And of course it's going to fail.

00:32:08.456 --> 00:32:11.652
So I think that we have too much,

00:32:11.652 --> 00:32:15.184
especially in the consulting
world or the design world,

00:32:15.184 --> 00:32:17.948
everybody is so paternalistic about it.

00:32:17.948 --> 00:32:19.931
And designers are famous for this.

00:32:19.931 --> 00:32:21.672
They get so personally offended

00:32:21.672 --> 00:32:25.665
when the client chooses the wrong thing.

00:32:25.665 --> 00:32:26.962
"God, they're such idiots!

00:32:26.962 --> 00:32:28.549
"Don't they know we're
trying to help them?"

00:32:28.549 --> 00:32:31.429
(audience laughs)

00:32:31.429 --> 00:32:32.512
Or who cares?

00:32:33.429 --> 00:32:35.380
It's their freaking business.

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:36.810
That's what you find.

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:37.643
I've had that.

00:32:37.643 --> 00:32:39.022
Instead of arguing with somebody

00:32:39.022 --> 00:32:40.657
for them to pick the right choice,

00:32:40.657 --> 00:32:42.658
'cause we genuinely want to help them,

00:32:42.658 --> 00:32:43.677
what I have found is

00:32:43.677 --> 00:32:45.035
if you push the
accountability down to them,

00:32:45.035 --> 00:32:47.756
'cause when we argue, we're
taking accountability.

00:32:47.756 --> 00:32:48.956
"This is better.

00:32:48.956 --> 00:32:50.994
"This will help you."

00:32:50.994 --> 00:32:53.533
We're taking responsibility,
accountability.

00:32:53.533 --> 00:32:55.388
But if we say, "Look.

00:32:55.388 --> 00:32:56.723
"We've been doing this a bunch of years.

00:32:56.723 --> 00:32:59.200
"We know more about design than you do.

00:32:59.200 --> 00:33:00.444
"I'm telling you,

00:33:00.444 --> 00:33:02.800
"for every reason that
I can outline for you,

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:04.742
"why this will help you more,

00:33:04.742 --> 00:33:07.014
"but if you don't want
to do it, that's fine.

00:33:07.014 --> 00:33:07.847
"It's your business.

00:33:07.847 --> 00:33:08.979
"Do what you want."

00:33:08.979 --> 00:33:10.501
The minute you switch the accountability

00:33:10.501 --> 00:33:11.767
and put it all on them,

00:33:11.767 --> 00:33:14.083
amazingly, they're much
more open to your opinion.

00:33:14.083 --> 00:33:15.723
(audience laughs)

00:33:15.723 --> 00:33:18.390
Because now they're responsible.

00:33:19.748 --> 00:33:21.541
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:33:21.541 --> 00:33:24.161
I made this video because
Anita asked me to.

00:33:24.161 --> 00:33:24.994
And if there's someone

00:33:24.994 --> 00:33:27.228
you'd like me to profile
in the next top 10,

00:33:27.228 --> 00:33:28.881
check out the link in the description

00:33:28.881 --> 00:33:30.727
and go and cast your vote.

00:33:30.727 --> 00:33:31.748
I'd also love to know,

00:33:31.748 --> 00:33:33.718
what was your favorite
message from this video?

00:33:33.718 --> 00:33:35.188
What did you learn from this video

00:33:35.188 --> 00:33:37.694
that you're going to
immediately apply somehow

00:33:37.694 --> 00:33:39.224
in your life or in you're business?

00:33:39.224 --> 00:33:40.667
Please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:33:40.667 --> 00:33:41.776
I'm really curious to find out.

00:33:41.776 --> 00:33:43.004
I also want to give a quick shoutout

00:33:43.004 --> 00:33:45.495
to the YouTube channel Obtain Eudaimonia.

00:33:45.495 --> 00:33:46.722
Thank you so much for picking up

00:33:46.722 --> 00:33:48.707
a copy of my book, Your One Word,

00:33:48.707 --> 00:33:51.816
doing that animated review,
and posting it to your channel.

00:33:51.816 --> 00:33:53.742
I really, really, really
appreciate the support

00:33:53.742 --> 00:33:55.939
and I'm so glad that you enjoyed the book.

00:33:55.939 --> 00:33:57.453
- [Narrator] His book explains the secret

00:33:57.453 --> 00:34:00.468
of how one word can transform
you and your business.

00:34:00.468 --> 00:34:01.942
- So thank you guys again for watching.

00:34:01.942 --> 00:34:03.271
I believe in you.

00:34:03.271 --> 00:34:05.226
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:34:05.226 --> 00:34:07.444
and whatever your one word is.

00:34:07.444 --> 00:34:08.787
Much love.

00:34:08.787 --> 00:34:10.167
I'll see you soon.

00:34:10.167 --> 00:34:12.667
(loud whoosh)

00:34:15.906 --> 00:34:17.472
- The greatest measure of human progress

00:34:17.472 --> 00:34:18.791
is working together.

00:34:18.791 --> 00:34:21.448
None of us can solve complex
problems by ourselves.

00:34:21.448 --> 00:34:23.532
None of us can lift a
heavy weight by ourselves.

00:34:23.532 --> 00:34:25.945
We can't build big things by ourselves.

00:34:25.945 --> 00:34:28.751
But when we learn to
cooperate and trust each other

00:34:28.751 --> 00:34:30.742
and share what we know with others,

00:34:30.742 --> 00:34:33.501
we work together and we can
solve the most complex problems,

00:34:33.501 --> 00:34:35.286
build the most remarkable things,

00:34:35.286 --> 00:34:36.497
and lift he heaviest of weights.

00:34:36.497 --> 00:34:38.697
So definitely, cooperation and trust.

00:34:38.697 --> 00:34:41.770
What I learned is that I don't
have to know all the answers,

00:34:41.770 --> 00:34:44.926
and if I don't, I don't
have to pretend that I do.

00:34:44.926 --> 00:34:46.764
The opportunity to ask others for help

00:34:46.764 --> 00:34:48.533
and admit when we don't know things

00:34:48.533 --> 00:34:50.524
and even admit when we make mistakes

00:34:50.524 --> 00:34:52.441
means that people will rush to our aid

00:34:52.441 --> 00:34:53.755
and will be there to help us.

00:34:53.755 --> 00:34:55.568
If we claim we know what the answer is,

00:34:55.568 --> 00:34:57.011
if we hide the mistakes we make,

00:34:57.011 --> 00:34:59.035
then people will think things are fine,

00:34:59.035 --> 00:35:00.750
and so they'll leave us alone.

00:35:00.750 --> 00:35:02.970
And it's not that people
don't want to help us.

00:35:02.970 --> 00:35:05.234
It's that they don't know when they can.

00:35:05.234 --> 00:35:06.482
I wake up every single morning

00:35:06.482 --> 00:35:07.786
with a very clear sense of purpose.

00:35:07.786 --> 00:35:10.678
It's to inspire people
to do what inspires them

00:35:10.678 --> 00:35:12.734
so together we can change our world.

00:35:12.734 --> 00:35:15.268
And I have a very clear vision
of the world I want to live in.

00:35:15.268 --> 00:35:16.735
I would like to live in a world in which

00:35:16.735 --> 00:35:19.804
the vast majority of people
wake up every single morning

00:35:19.804 --> 00:35:22.741
inspired to go to work, they
feel safe when they're there,

00:35:22.741 --> 00:35:24.021
and they come home at the end of the day

00:35:24.021 --> 00:35:25.753
fulfilled by the work that they do.

00:35:25.753 --> 00:35:27.828
And I've committed all of my work,

00:35:27.828 --> 00:35:29.522
and it doesn't matter what form it takes,

00:35:29.522 --> 00:35:32.273
whether it's speaking or
writing or teaching or advising

00:35:32.273 --> 00:35:34.311
or it doesn't even matter
what form it takes,

00:35:34.311 --> 00:35:37.048
in some way, shape, or form
to help advance that vision

00:35:37.048 --> 00:35:39.179
and move closer and closer to it.

00:35:39.179 --> 00:35:41.480
My friends are my allies.

00:35:41.480 --> 00:35:42.512
We're part of an army

00:35:42.512 --> 00:35:45.024
that are trying to build
this world together.

00:35:45.024 --> 00:35:46.957
That's why I come to
conferences like this.

00:35:46.957 --> 00:35:49.247
It gives me a bully pulpit
to share that vision

00:35:49.247 --> 00:35:51.542
with the hope that some of
the companies in the room

00:35:51.542 --> 00:35:52.803
will start to change the way

00:35:52.803 --> 00:35:54.500
they at least view what they're doing

00:35:54.500 --> 00:35:55.970
and change the way they do things.

00:35:55.970 --> 00:35:58.261
Because I'm just a preacher.

00:35:58.261 --> 00:35:59.530
That's all I am.

00:35:59.530 --> 00:36:01.777
Unless companies actually
affect the change,

00:36:01.777 --> 00:36:03.532
my vision will never come to life.

00:36:03.532 --> 00:36:04.488
So well I know that.

00:36:04.488 --> 00:36:06.291
So it's going to take a lot of us,

00:36:06.291 --> 00:36:07.692
and we all play a different part.

00:36:07.692 --> 00:36:09.320
We all play pieces of a jigsaw puzzle

00:36:09.320 --> 00:36:11.712
out of which I am just one piece.

